WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.256 --> 00:00:04.779
Hello! Welcome to a new Project Kamp&nbsp;update!

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:07.621
This week, it has been very busy at the Kamp.

00:00:07.621 --> 00:00:11.103
Now, in this video, we will show you&nbsp;
three improvements we have been working on.

00:00:11.103 --> 00:00:17.139
We're going to start building our Level 2&nbsp;house,
 continue with our never-ending task of freeing the native trees...

00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:19.696
And, we will do a cool upcycling project!

00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:24.692
So, there is a lot of stuff to do,
 and the first thing is to start building the Level 2 house.

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:26.075
Here we go!

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.446
- In previous updates, you saw bringing&nbsp;this pig trailer to our land.

00:00:31.446 --> 00:00:36.358
Then, we saw the state of it, 
took out what was not usable anymore and repaired it.

00:00:36.358 --> 00:00:40.620
Now, we have a clean structure to work on,
 so we are going&nbsp;to bring the materials and start building!

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.227
In order to start the interior of the new Level 2 house,
we have to build a wooden&nbsp;frame.

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:58.241
Just so we have a base easier to build on 
where&nbsp;we can put the insulation, the electrics and more.

00:00:58.241 --> 00:01:00.507
For that, we are using these battens.

00:01:00.507 --> 00:01:02.968
They will&nbsp;be attached to the steel frame of the trailer.

00:01:02.968 --> 00:01:07.740
They'll be the transition between the exterior&nbsp;steel frame
 and the interior wooden frame.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.403
- Here, we have these battens.

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:16.017
We all have to&nbsp;cut them in a little angle.

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:23.022
It's because we put them in here,
in between the steel and the&nbsp;rest of the wood.

00:01:23.022 --> 00:01:25.999
The water coming from up there can run off.

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:31.440
And, this has the same thickness as the&nbsp;tube,
so we have a nice flat surface to work on!

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:28.080
- Good enough.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.847
We've put these lovely battens in.

00:02:34.847 --> 00:02:38.280
We have cut out the doors.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.727
And, the windows...

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.436
- Windows.

00:02:45.436 --> 00:02:50.762
So, yeah, we have to close all the battens on the back.

00:02:50.762 --> 00:02:53.539
Just to be able to foil&nbsp;everything.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:54.654
Teamwork!

00:02:54.654 --> 00:02:57.120
One foiling, other ones--

00:02:58.164 --> 00:02:59.208
- Battening.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.400
I don't know if it's gonna hold.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.180
Oh yeah! It seems to fit!

00:03:16.552 --> 00:03:18.074
We've put all the&nbsp;battens in.

00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:21.188
Before we can keep on going...

00:03:22.232 --> 00:03:27.720
The door, the entrance door, is going here,
 so we have to cut.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.440
Like butter!

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.405
I got a new antenna!

00:03:56.496 --> 00:04:04.511
- So, we just bought the foil,
and now we can put it up with some OSB planks.

00:04:09.737 --> 00:04:12.561
The water shouldn't get in.

00:04:12.561 --> 00:04:16.560
- We're going&nbsp;to put it in between this stuff and the wall.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.031
It's waterproof, but it's still breathable.

00:04:20.031 --> 00:04:22.860
If water comes in, it can get out, but...

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.105
Most of the water cannot get in.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.835
It's an important&nbsp;layer for the durability of structure.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.631
- The foil is done!

00:04:51.631 --> 00:04:54.281
The next step is to attach these OSB boards.

00:04:54.281 --> 00:04:57.541
These boards are made out of compressed leftover wood chips.

00:04:57.541 --> 00:05:00.710
We decided to use them, because then the foil is supported.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.364
So, it doesn't break and it stays in place in the long run.

00:05:03.364 --> 00:05:07.872
We know it's not the most sustainable material, but it's thin and light.

00:05:08.418 --> 00:05:11.166
There are&nbsp;no many more options available in the sawmill...

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.460
Now, we are cutting the holes&nbsp;where the windows will go.

00:06:13.008 --> 00:06:14.310
- Okay, one more...

00:06:17.158 --> 00:06:19.811
So, the OSB is in.

00:06:19.811 --> 00:06:20.825
The foil's in.

00:06:21.401 --> 00:06:27.981
The next thing we want to do is get&nbsp;the foil 
and put this to the outside of the steel as much as possible

00:06:28.527 --> 00:06:32.421
Because now, the&nbsp;water can run along the foil.

00:06:32.421 --> 00:06:38.980
If there is a leak, it then will enter somewhere 
in between&nbsp;the steel and the wooden frame.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:44.940
We're going to take it out as much as possible, 
and&nbsp;try to flap it a bit over the steel beam.

00:07:01.397 --> 00:07:05.313
So, the first beam of the walls will be straight&nbsp;on the steel.

00:07:05.313 --> 00:07:06.604
You can see the ground here.

00:07:06.604 --> 00:07:09.267
It's really the bottom of the truck, and it's on the cold steel...

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.414
We want to get some oil on it, to make it a bit more durable.

00:07:13.414 --> 00:07:15.808
Because, it can be a humid&nbsp;place in there.

00:07:15.808 --> 00:07:19.168
- After oiling these beams, we screw them into the steel frame.

00:07:19.168 --> 00:07:22.500
This is the new&nbsp;base, which the wooden frame is going to sit on.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.120
- That was good.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.696
Okay, now we can start framing.

00:07:53.696 --> 00:07:56.100
So, the&nbsp;first step is to build the window frames.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.090
My problem is with the heat.

00:08:17.846 --> 00:08:21.446
- For a million dollars, would you live--

00:08:21.446 --> 00:08:25.265
Robert is interested in a million dollar question.

00:08:25.265 --> 00:08:30.517
- For a million dollars, would you want 
to survive one year in the Ice Age?

00:08:30.517 --> 00:08:31.985
In the Ice Age?

00:08:31.985 --> 00:08:34.182
- Yeah, like&nbsp;with mammoths, and--

00:08:34.182 --> 00:08:35.054
That would be cool!

00:08:35.054 --> 00:08:35.850
- A T-Rex.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:36.350
Yeah!

00:08:38.676 --> 00:08:39.830
Can I bring my tools with me?

00:08:40.315 --> 00:08:41.135
- Yes,&nbsp;you can!

00:08:41.135 --> 00:08:43.901
Yeah? Just woodwork over there...

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.614
I'm good where I am right now!

00:08:46.323 --> 00:08:47.929
I don't need a million dollars!

00:08:50.204 --> 00:08:56.984
The two windows are framed out,
so&nbsp;right now we're gonna put them in place.

00:09:20.987 --> 00:09:22.113
That's good!

00:09:26.426 --> 00:09:30.314
Then, we kept&nbsp;on building the rest of the frame,
 but we lost some footage...

00:09:30.314 --> 00:09:31.920
So, here's a timelapse of it.

00:09:56.149 --> 00:09:57.633
- Is it hot up there?

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.660
You can't feel it watching the videos on YouTube, but...

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.435
It's 10 o'clock in the morning, and it&nbsp;feels like 40 degrees already.

00:10:11.435 --> 00:10:12.391
But, hey!

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.035
I can't say anything.

00:10:17.399 --> 00:10:18.724
I'm having fun working!

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:21.808
With Robert behind the camera, and working with me.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:27.623
- Yes, sir.

00:10:27.623 --> 00:10:29.375
We are just positive!

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.500
This is our last piece of the framing.

00:10:38.783 --> 00:10:41.227
It's starting to be not doable anymore...

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.140
I mean, for sure, I don't look&nbsp;good,
 but you don't look better.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:00.761
It's shaking!

00:11:00.761 --> 00:11:02.235
My hand is fucking shaking!

00:11:02.235 --> 00:11:03.516
There,&nbsp;that's better.

00:11:05.993 --> 00:11:07.620
Framing finished, Robert-o!

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.034
The walls are all done!

00:11:12.792 --> 00:11:15.783
So, yeah, we can start&nbsp;with the with the roof.

00:11:15.783 --> 00:11:16.791
- So, we get some shade!

00:11:16.791 --> 00:11:20.311
But, I guess we're gonna wait until tomorrow...

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.832
We'll keep on doing stuff tomorrow, because it's not&nbsp;doable.

00:11:27.408 --> 00:11:28.605
I'm sweaty.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.576
- The new house is starting to take shape,
 but it's too hot to continue today!

00:11:34.576 --> 00:11:37.475
So,&nbsp;we will continue building in the next update.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.187
Okay, that was the pig trailer.

00:11:41.187 --> 00:11:44.584
Now, we're gonna go to&nbsp;the fields,
because we will clear some mimosas over there.

00:11:44.584 --> 00:11:48.233
Just to make the roads nice and tidy,
 so, we'll move&nbsp;on to the next chapter!

00:11:48.233 --> 00:11:51.444
Right here is the main road, that crosses our land.

00:11:51.444 --> 00:11:56.688
We need to clean it, because if there is a wildfire,
 we will need to use this road to escape.

00:11:56.688 --> 00:11:59.917
And, having all these mimosas&nbsp;around means a potential risk.

00:11:59.917 --> 00:12:02.529
They could catch fire, and make it dangerous to drive away.

00:12:02.529 --> 00:12:05.340
Or, even&nbsp;worse, they could burn and fall, blocking the road.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.857
Today, we will be working on this side of&nbsp;the road.

00:12:08.857 --> 00:12:12.394
Here, you basically find mimosa trees and some oaks.

00:12:12.394 --> 00:12:17.333
As we are still in the&nbsp;fire season here in Portugal,
we can only use power tools in the early morning.

00:12:17.333 --> 00:12:19.870
So, it's time to start.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.579
- Are you ready to 'bramble'?

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.020
These last three of this patch a bit big.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:54.960
We are a bit worried about the ruin.

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:59.640
So, let's try to see if we can carry on,
without breaking the whole Community Center.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.880
Somehow, even though we just filleted 
a&nbsp;bunch of trees, this pomegranate made it.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.962
- It's always annoying to work with brambles,
which are spiky&nbsp;and entangled.

00:15:05.962 --> 00:15:07.995
But, not everything is bad!

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:40.250
- We've reached a bit of a precarious situation&nbsp;here.

00:15:40.826 --> 00:15:47.250
We have a ton of these dead and burnt trees
 that are sort of interwoven together,&nbsp;especially at their crowns.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:51.391
And, it makes it very hard to 
pull everything down and clear it out.

00:15:51.391 --> 00:15:57.413
So, our plan of action for this will be to send Màtè in,
 because I don't want to chainsaw and get&nbsp;covered by ants.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:04.318
We will try to cut as many of these at the bottom, 
and hopefully the weight of&nbsp;all these trees will come tumbling down.

00:16:04.318 --> 00:16:06.702
Or, we'll have to pull them out ourselves.

00:16:06.702 --> 00:16:09.000
But, hopefully,&nbsp;this should be an easy way to deal with this.

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.334
- We're almost finished guys!

00:17:26.334 --> 00:17:27.484
How do you feel?

00:17:28.636 --> 00:17:31.761
- Just about&nbsp;as excited as you sound.

00:17:32.815 --> 00:17:33.640
Fresh!

00:17:34.613 --> 00:17:35.742
- What's the problem?

00:17:35.742 --> 00:17:39.520
My hands are vibrating from&nbsp;the chainsaw.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.220
I feel like shaking!

00:17:42.660 --> 00:17:44.367
But, it's done.

00:17:44.367 --> 00:17:46.140
So, no more chainsaw for a while at least.

00:17:56.460 --> 00:18:02.249
So, now that we cleared this whole area,
it's very&nbsp;important that we can maintain it.

00:18:02.249 --> 00:18:05.491
To maintain it, we do it mostly with the brush cutter.

00:18:05.491 --> 00:18:10.593
So, we're&nbsp;gonna make this brush cutter proof.
 and remove all the brambles.

00:18:10.593 --> 00:18:15.268
Also, the higher stems: we're&nbsp;gonna cut them down,
 so we can access it more easily.

00:18:15.268 --> 00:18:18.180
- But, before that, let me introduce you&nbsp;to Debbie.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:19.760
Hey! My name is Debbie!

00:18:20.457 --> 00:18:28.267
I'm from the Netherlands, but I'm currently living and traveling&nbsp;
with my love Wouter, our sun Siep and our dog Otis.

00:18:28.267 --> 00:18:29.201
I know right!

00:18:29.201 --> 00:18:30.726
That escalated quickly.

00:18:30.726 --> 00:18:33.712
I'm a yoga teacher and massage therapist.

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:39.877
With a big passion for humans and their crazy&nbsp;
monkey minds, music and growing my own food.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:44.192
I came to Project Kamp to learn more about&nbsp;living in a community.

00:18:44.192 --> 00:18:48.205
Sharing resources and getting inspired by like-minded people.

00:18:48.205 --> 00:18:52.437
For&nbsp;the future, I hope to find a beautiful 
piece of land in the north of Spain.

00:18:52.437 --> 00:18:53.781
Where we can&nbsp;bring people together...

00:18:53.781 --> 00:19:01.145
And, hopefully, to have a positive influence in a world
 that sometimes&nbsp;feels so fast and stressful for so many people.

00:19:42.834 --> 00:19:44.213
- What do you got there?

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.740
A big barbed wire!

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:49.500
B.B.W.

00:19:52.155 --> 00:19:55.105
- As you can see, the gloves that&nbsp;
you saw us repairing some time&nbsp;ago...

00:19:55.105 --> 00:19:56.593
They're holding up quite well.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.100
- This is what we're talking&nbsp;about!

00:20:07.464 --> 00:20:10.424
A lot of barbed wire.

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:16.243
- That's not our tractor...

00:20:16.243 --> 00:20:18.043
It's Antonio, our neighbor.

00:20:18.043 --> 00:20:20.280
He came to grab some wood that we just chopped.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:24.318
Because, we don't really need it all,
so we always offer it.

00:20:25.137 --> 00:20:27.452
- And then, he's gonna use it for fire in the winter?

00:20:27.452 --> 00:20:28.518
Yes!

00:20:28.518 --> 00:20:29.833
- That's amazing!

00:21:12.383 --> 00:21:13.840
So, it's done.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.110
Antonio now has a lot of&nbsp;wood.

00:21:17.557 --> 00:21:18.852
- Perfect!

00:21:19.848 --> 00:21:22.589
We still have some thin branches laying around, but we called Bruno.

00:21:22.589 --> 00:21:24.128
He has a wood chipper.

00:21:24.128 --> 00:21:28.918
So, we chop all the wood
 and spread it around the base of the&nbsp;cut mimosas.

00:21:28.918 --> 00:21:30.360
They'll struggle to sprout again.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:34.706
Now, this space looks way bigger!

00:21:36.191 --> 00:21:38.280
And, more safe, in case of a fire.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.785
This side is done, but the other side still needs&nbsp;some work.

00:21:42.785 --> 00:21:45.998
And, it's the one with more variety of native trees.

00:21:45.998 --> 00:21:47.994
But, we'll continue this work later!

00:21:49.297 --> 00:21:50.174
All right!

00:21:50.174 --> 00:21:52.809
So, that was the mimosa clearing.

00:21:52.809 --> 00:21:57.187
The next part of the video is actually going to be 
about&nbsp;these chairs that I'm sitting in.

00:21:57.187 --> 00:22:00.463
They have something to do with that blue tent over&nbsp;there.

00:22:00.463 --> 00:22:02.100
But, Kevin can tell you more about this!

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:12.000
- So, we have this collection of broken down chairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.405
Just laying around by the pizza oven...

00:22:17.204 --> 00:22:20.337
I think&nbsp;they would need a bit of love.

00:22:20.337 --> 00:22:25.251
Since we have some tarp left and they are good for outdoor...

00:22:25.251 --> 00:22:28.146
I&nbsp;think we can fix the chairs with the tarp.

00:22:28.146 --> 00:22:28.980
Let's see!

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.442
The first step is to remove the old fabric.

00:22:35.442 --> 00:22:39.000
We will try&nbsp;to keep it and use it as a mold for the new seats.

00:22:59.560 --> 00:23:05.695
The blue top I'm using is the leftover&nbsp;for fixing the white tent,
 that you saw in&nbsp;the previous update.

00:23:05.695 --> 00:23:08.229
So, we cut the fabric up.

00:23:09.453 --> 00:23:12.840
Now, we need to trace it on the tarp and cut it up.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:38.160
The tarp is quite strong, it requires several pass to cut it,
but this also will last very long!

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:48.013
The first two are quite simple!

00:23:48.013 --> 00:23:49.487
They're just a&nbsp;rectangular shape.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:52.173
The third one is a bit tricky.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:48.960
This hole will be needed to fold
&nbsp;the top around the metal frame.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:22.265
Sewing time!

00:25:22.265 --> 00:25:26.160
Let's see if we can manage with&nbsp;this big tarp 
without breaking any needles.

00:25:31.609 --> 00:25:34.640
For the first two chairs, I only need to sew the&nbsp;end.

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:37.920
To make a sleeve for the metal&nbsp;rod 
that will attach to the frame.

00:25:55.229 --> 00:25:56.492
One is done!

00:25:56.492 --> 00:25:59.108
The second chair is exactly the same process.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:21.120
That's too loose...

00:26:55.399 --> 00:26:56.540
The third chair is different.

00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:59.640
The top will&nbsp;attach to the frame using some Velcro.

00:27:04.184 --> 00:27:08.864
You can also see that I reinforced
&nbsp;the hole with some extra tarp.

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:46.750
Finally, I managed to put it&nbsp;in.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:50.460
As a last thing, I'm gonna put some bolts 
to make sure the top stays in place.

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:21.611
Okay...

00:28:21.611 --> 00:28:23.243
Now, we have some new chairs!

00:28:23.243 --> 00:28:29.280
And, we&nbsp;have reused some leftover material
 from the white tent renovation.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.380
We saved this three very&nbsp;comfortable chairs
 from ending up in the junkyard.

00:28:40.817 --> 00:28:43.201
- Okay Kevin! Thank you very much!

00:28:43.201 --> 00:28:46.046
They are very&nbsp;comfy chairs, but we have no much time to sit in this

00:28:46.046 --> 00:28:47.784
Because, we're moving on to the next video!

00:28:47.784 --> 00:28:50.105
Next week, we are going talk about the challenges.

00:28:50.105 --> 00:28:53.002
So, if you want to ask any questions,
 put them in the&nbsp;comments.

00:28:53.002 --> 00:28:58.276
And, if you like these videos, support us on Patreon 
and you can see them one week ahead!

00:28:58.276 --> 00:29:00.683
If not, we'll see you next week on the same channel.

00:29:00.683 --> 00:29:01.440
Ciao!

